# Project planning, step 7: a reusable template

A project you run more than once can become a template that you, or anyone you share it with, can start again and again. Two things make this work:
* The `!` marks say which values are part of the plan, and are copied into every new project. Everything else starts blank.
* Relative dates, such as `+2w`, are turned into real dates counted from the day the project is started.

The only change to the structure is the `!` marks on the names, descriptions and dates of projects and sub-projects. Tickets already copy their names, descriptions and dates, because the built-in TaskItem marks them.

```bss
t Project|upj
    A project: what you are delivering, broken down into sub-projects and tickets.
    s+!                          Name|Project|A short name for the project
    s!                           Description|Description|What the project will deliver, and why
    dd!                          StartDate|Starts|When the project starts
    dd!|(DueDate >= StartDate)   DueDate|Due|When the project should be finished
    r:tu=me                      Owner|Owner|Who is responsible for the project
    l:usp                        SubProjects|Sub-projects|The parts of the project
    l:uti                        Tickets|Tickets|Work that belongs to the whole project
    l:tcm                        Comments|Comments|Notes and discussion

t SubProject|usp
    A part of a project, such as a release or a workstream. It can have sub-projects of its own.
    s+!       Name|Sub-project|A short name for this part of the project
    s!        Description|Description|What this part delivers
    l:usp     SubProjects|Sub-projects|Smaller parts of this one
    l:uti     Tickets|Tickets|The work in this part
    l:tcm     Comments|Comments|Notes and discussion

x Ticket:TaskItem|uti
    A piece of work in a project. A big ticket can be broken down into sub-tickets.
    l:uti     SubTasks|Sub-tickets|Smaller tickets that make up this one
    l:r:uti   DependsOn|Depends On|Tickets that must be done before this one
```
